/* Professor Stick Figure's diagnostics.
   Each question: t = topic, k = track ("cl" common law, "ucc", or "both"),
   q = question, o = options, a = index of the correct option, w = why. */
window.QUIZZES = [
{
  id: 1,
  title: "Deal or No Deal",
  blurb: "Which rulebook applies, and did a contract ever form?",
  covers: "Sources of law · consideration · offer · acceptance · reliance",
  questions: [
    { t: "Which rulebook?", k: "both",
      q: "A homeowner hired a flooring company to supply oak planks for $6,500 and to lay them for $900. The planks were flawless, but the installer left gaps between the boards. The homeowner sued for breach of contract in a state that applies the predominant purpose test. Select the option that best describes which source of law governs the dispute:",
      o: ["The common law, because the gravamen of the complaint is the faulty installation.",
          "The UCC, because the contract as a whole is predominantly a sale of goods.",
          "The UCC, because the flooring company is a merchant that deals in goods of the kind.",
          "The common law, because planks fixed to a house are land and not goods."],
      a: 1,
      w: "The predominant purpose test asks what the deal is mainly about, and here nearly all of the money is for the planks. So the UCC governs the whole contract, even the installation part. Pointing to the faulty installation is the gravamen of the complaint test, which this state does not use." },
    { t: "Which rulebook?", k: "both",
      q: "Which of the following statements is least accurate about the sources of contract law?",
      o: ["Article 2 of the UCC is a statute enacted in every state except Louisiana.",
          "Where the UCC is silent on a point, the common law fills the gap.",
          "The Restatement (Second) of Contracts is persuasive authority that courts often follow.",
          "Article 2 of the UCC applies only when at least one party to the sale is a merchant."],
      a: 3,
      w: "Article 2 covers every sale of goods, between anyone, even two neighbours selling a bike. Being a merchant only matters for a few special rules inside the UCC. The other three statements are accurate: the UCC is a binding statute, the common law fills its gaps, and the Restatement persuades but does not bind." },
    { t: "Which rulebook?", k: "ucc",
      q: "Which of the following is NOT a \"good\" under UCC § 2-105?",
      o: ["Shares of stock in a family corporation.",
          "A printing press specially manufactured to the buyer's design.",
          "A crop of soybeans still growing in the seller's field.",
          "The unborn calf of a dairy cow."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Goods are things that are movable when they are identified to the contract. Shares and other investment securities are left out of that definition. The custom press is the tempting choice, but specially manufactured goods are still goods, and so are growing crops and the unborn young of animals." },
    { t: "The Bargain", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following is the most accurate definition of consideration?",
      o: ["The careful thought a party gives to a deal before deciding to enter it.",
          "A detriment suffered by a promisee who reasonably relied on a promise.",
          "A performance or return promise that is bargained for, sought by the promisor and given in exchange.",
          "An exchange of roughly equal value that a court finds fair to both sides."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Under Restatement § 71, consideration is a performance or a return promise that is bargained for. Each side's piece is the price of the other's. Detriment suffered in reliance describes promissory estoppel, which is a different doctrine, and courts do not ask whether the exchange was equal or fair." },
    { t: "The Bargain", k: "cl",
      q: "A mechanic driving home saw a stranded motorist, a man of 30, and towed his car forty miles to town without being asked and without charge. A week later the motorist's mother, who had known nothing of the breakdown, wrote to the mechanic: \"For what you did for my son, I will pay you $300.\" She never paid. If the mechanic sues the mother:",
      o: ["The mechanic will win, because the mother had a moral obligation to pay for her son's rescue.",
          "The mechanic will win, because he relied on the mother's promise when he towed the car.",
          "The mother will win, because the towing was finished before she promised and so was not bargained for.",
          "The mother will win, because a promise to pay for a benefit to another person is void."],
      a: 2,
      w: "The tow was already done when the mother made her promise, so it could not be the price of that promise. Past consideration is no consideration, which is the lesson of Mills and Wyman. A moral obligation alone does not make a contract, and the mechanic could not have relied on a promise that did not exist yet." },
    { t: "The Bargain", k: "cl",
      q: "In which of the following is a court most likely to find consideration?",
      o: ["A grandmother promises her grandson $2,000 if he stays off social media until graduation, and he does.",
          "A manager promises a retiring clerk a $5,000 bonus \"in recognition of your twenty years of service.\"",
          "A woman tells her neighbor, \"Come by my garage on Sunday and you can have my old bicycle.\"",
          "A brother promises to drive his sister to the airport, and she then books an early flight."],
      a: 0,
      w: "The grandson gave up something he had a legal right to do because his grandmother asked for it, and forbearance that is bargained for is consideration, as in Hamer and Sidway. The bonus for twenty years of service looks valuable, but that work was already done, so it is past consideration. The bicycle is a gift with a condition, and the airport ride is a gratuitous promise." },
    { t: "The Offer", k: "cl",
      q: "At a colleague's retirement party, Dale complained loudly about his vintage motorcycle and told a coworker, \"It's yours for $2,000.\" The coworker asked whether the helmet was included. Dale said yes, wrote \"I sell my motorcycle and helmet to Priya for $2,000\" on a card, signed it and handed it over. Priya said, \"Deal,\" and signed it too. The next morning Priya brought $2,000 in cash. Dale refused, explaining that he had been joking the whole time and never meant to sell. Nothing in his manner at the party suggested a joke. Which best describes the legal situation?",
      o: ["There is no contract, because Dale did not subjectively intend to sell the motorcycle.",
          "There is no contract, because a statement made at a social gathering is a gratuitous promise.",
          "There is a contract, because Priya relied on the card by withdrawing the cash.",
          "There is a contract, because under the objective theory of assent Dale's words and conduct showed a serious offer."],
      a: 3,
      w: "The law looks at what a person shows, not at what he secretly thinks. Dale negotiated a term, wrote the deal down and signed it, so a reasonable person would take him as serious, just as in Lucy and Zehmer. His private joke does not count, and there is no need to reach for reliance, because there was an offer, an acceptance and a price." },
    { t: "The Offer", k: "cl",
      q: "A furniture store mailed a flyer reading: \"Spring clearance! Leather recliners from $199. Great selection, while supplies last.\" A customer arrived with $199 in cash and demanded a leather recliner. The cheapest recliner left in the store was priced at $450, and the store refused to sell it for less. If the customer sues:",
      o: ["The customer will win, because the flyer was an offer that she accepted by tendering the price.",
          "The store will win, because the flyer was an invitation to deal and not a clear, definite offer.",
          "The store will win, because the offeror is master of the offer and may change the price at will.",
          "The customer will win, because a merchant's advertisement is a firm offer that cannot be revoked."],
      a: 1,
      w: "An advertisement is usually only an invitation to deal. It becomes an offer only when it is clear, definite and leaves nothing to negotiate, like the one fur stole for one dollar in Lefkowitz. This flyer names no particular recliner and no quantity, so there was no offer for the customer to accept. Master of the offer is the wrong reason, because that rule is about who may accept an offer and how." },
    { t: "The Offer", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following is least likely to be treated as an offer?",
      o: ["A poster promising \"$200 to whoever returns my lost terrier, Biscuit.\"",
          "A signed letter of intent listing a price but stating it is \"subject to a final written agreement.\"",
          "A signed note reading, \"I will sell you my canoe for $350. Tell me by Sunday.\"",
          "An advertisement reading, \"Saturday 8 a.m.: one 55-inch television, $50, first person through the door.\""],
      a: 1,
      w: "An offer shows willingness to deal so that the other person's yes will close the bargain. Words like \"subject to a final written agreement\" tell the reader that the parties are still negotiating, which was the point of Empro and Ball-Co. The television advertisement is tempting because advertisements are usually not offers, but this one is clear, definite and leaves nothing to negotiate." },
    { t: "Acceptance", k: "cl",
      q: "On June 1 a landowner sent a prospective buyer a signed letter offering to sell a vacant parcel for $90,000. The letter added, \"This offer will stay open until June 30.\" The buyer paid nothing for that assurance. On June 20 the landowner telephoned the buyer and said, \"I've changed my mind. The offer is withdrawn.\" On June 25 the buyer hand-delivered a signed letter accepting the offer at $90,000. Which best describes the result?",
      o: ["There is a contract, because the landowner promised to keep the offer open until June 30.",
          "There is a contract, because the revocation of a written offer must itself be in writing.",
          "There is no contract, because the offer had already lapsed after a reasonable time.",
          "There is no contract, because the promise to hold the offer open was gratuitous and the offer was revoked first."],
      a: 3,
      w: "A promise to keep an offer open binds only if the offeree gives consideration for it. Nothing was paid here, so the landowner was free to revoke at any time before acceptance, and the phone call on June 20 did that. That is the rule from Dickinson and Dodds. The offer did not lapse, because it named its own deadline, so lapse of time is the wrong reason." },
    { t: "Acceptance", k: "cl",
      q: "Same facts as the previous question: the landowner's June 1 letter offered the parcel for $90,000 and said the offer would stay open until June 30, the landowner telephoned on June 20 to withdraw it, and the buyer delivered a signed acceptance on June 25. This time, however, the buyer had paid the landowner $200 on June 1 in exchange for the promise to keep the offer open. Which best describes the result?",
      o: ["There is no contract, because the offeror is master of the offer and may revoke before acceptance.",
          "There is no contract, because $200 is inadequate consideration for tying up a $90,000 parcel.",
          "There is a contract, because the $200 created an option contract and the attempted revocation had no effect.",
          "There is a contract, because the buyer relied on the deadline and reliance takes the place of acceptance."],
      a: 2,
      w: "This time the buyer bought the time. Consideration given for a promise to hold an offer open creates an option contract, so the offer could not be revoked before June 30 and the June 25 acceptance formed a contract. The $200 is enough, because courts do not weigh the adequacy of consideration." },
    { t: "Acceptance", k: "cl",
      q: "On March 1 a seller mailed a buyer an offer to sell a piano. The offer said nothing about how or when acceptance had to be made. On March 4 the seller mailed a revocation, which the buyer received on March 6. On March 5 the buyer mailed a properly addressed acceptance, which the seller received on March 8. Which analysis is most accurate?",
      o: ["A contract was formed on March 5, because an acceptance is effective when sent and the revocation had not yet arrived.",
          "No contract was formed, because the revocation was sent on March 4, before the acceptance was sent.",
          "A contract was formed on March 8, because an acceptance is effective when the offeror receives it.",
          "No contract was formed, because under the mirror image rule letters that cross in the mail cancel out."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Under the mailbox rule in Restatement § 63, a properly addressed acceptance takes effect the moment it is sent. A revocation works only when it is received. The buyer's acceptance went out on March 5, a day before the revocation landed, so the contract was formed that day. Looking at the date the revocation was mailed is the classic mistake." },
    { t: "Promissory Estoppel", k: "cl",
      q: "An uncle, proud that his nephew had been admitted to culinary school, told him, \"I am giving you $15,000 toward tuition. No strings attached.\" The uncle knew the nephew could not attend without the money. Trusting the promise, the nephew quit his job and signed a nonrefundable enrollment agreement. The uncle then refused to pay. Which best describes the most likely outcome?",
      o: ["The nephew will win, because quitting his job was consideration for the uncle's promise.",
          "The uncle will win, because a gratuitous promise cannot be enforced on any theory.",
          "The uncle will win, because the nephew never communicated an acceptance of the promise.",
          "The nephew will win, because the uncle should have expected the reliance and the nephew relied to his detriment."],
      a: 3,
      w: "The uncle asked for nothing in return, so this is a gratuitous promise with no consideration. Quitting the job was not the price of the promise, so calling it consideration is the wrong reason. But under Restatement § 90 a promise that the promisor should expect to cause reliance, and that does cause it, can be enforced to avoid injustice, as in Ricketts and Scothorn." },
    { t: "Promissory Estoppel", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following facts is most likely to defeat a claim of promissory estoppel under Restatement § 90?",
      o: ["The defendant said only, \"We are thinking about bringing you on next year.\"",
          "The defendant's promise was spoken and was never put in writing.",
          "The defendant received no benefit from what the plaintiff did.",
          "The plaintiff gave nothing and promised nothing in return for the defendant's promise."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Promissory estoppel starts with a real promise, and reliance on it must be reasonable. \"We are thinking about it\" is a maybe, not a promise. The lack of anything given in return is the tempting choice, but that only shows there was no consideration, which is exactly when § 90 is used as plan B." },
    { t: "Promissory Estoppel", k: "cl",
      q: "A developer told a caterer, \"The cafe space in my new building is yours. Start getting ready to open.\" Both understood that they would not be bound to a lease until one was signed, and none ever was. Trusting the developer, the caterer paid $9,000 for custom counters that fit only that space and have no resale value, and $3,000 for permits. The caterer had projected a first-year profit of $40,000. The developer then leased the space to someone else. If a court finds for the caterer on promissory estoppel and awards the regular measure of damages for that claim, the caterer will recover:",
      o: ["$52,000",
          "$40,000",
          "$12,000",
          "$28,000"],
      a: 2,
      w: "The regular measure for promissory estoppel is reliance damages: what you lost by trusting the promise, not what you hoped to gain. The caterer spent $9,000 on counters and $3,000 on permits, which comes to $12,000. The $40,000 of projected profit is expectation, the benefit of the bargain, and as in the Aretha Franklin case that is not what § 90 usually gives." }
  ]
},
{
  id: 2,
  title: "The Fine Print",
  blurb: "What did you agree to, and what counts as part of the deal?",
  covers: "Boilerplate · online contracts · battle of the forms · interpretation · parol evidence · statute of frauds",
  questions: [
    { t: "Boilerplate", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following is the least accurate statement about standard form contracts under Restatement § 211?",
      o: ["A party who signs a standard form adopts its terms whether or not the party read them.",
          "A standard form is interpreted the same way for every customer, whatever each one knew of its terms.",
          "A term is not part of the deal if the drafter had reason to believe the signer would refuse it if aware of it.",
          "A contract of adhesion is void because the customer had no chance to negotiate its terms."],
      a: 3,
      w: "A contract of adhesion is a take-it-or-leave-it form, and it is not void just for that reason. Under § 211 the signer adopts the terms, read or not, and the form means the same thing for every customer. The only escape in that section is a term the drafter had reason to believe you would never have accepted." },
    { t: "Boilerplate", k: "cl",
      q: "A rafting company based in Colorado sells trips to customers across the country. The booking form each customer receives before paying states that any lawsuit against the company must be filed in Colorado. A customer from Ohio, who saw the clause but had no chance to negotiate it, is injured on a trip and sues in Ohio. What is the most likely result?",
      o: ["The clause will be enforced, because a reasonable forum clause with notice binds even without negotiation.",
          "The clause will be enforced, because under the last shot rule the company's form controls.",
          "The clause will not be enforced, because forum clauses bind only sophisticated parties who bargained over them.",
          "The clause will not be enforced, because a contract of adhesion is voidable at the customer's election."],
      a: 0,
      w: "This follows Carnival Cruise Lines and Shute. A forum selection clause in a form contract is enforced when it is reasonable and the customer had notice, because a company with customers everywhere has a real interest in one home court. The tempting wrong answer comes from The Bremen, where the parties were bargaining equals, but Carnival extended the rule to forms nobody negotiated." },
    { t: "Online Contracts", k: "cl",
      q: "What is the least accurate statement about assent to online terms?",
      o: ["Clickwrap is usually enforced because clicking \"I agree\" is an outward manifestation of assent.",
          "Browsewrap binds a user who knew, or reasonably should have known, that the terms existed.",
          "A user who clicked \"I agree\" can escape the terms by proving that he never actually read them.",
          "Inquiry notice, without actual knowledge of the terms, can be enough to bind a user."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Under the duty to read, a user who clicks on something that is plainly an agreement is bound whether or not he read it. Assent is judged from the outside, so the question is notice plus an act showing agreement, not what the user actually studied. The other three statements are accurate descriptions of clickwrap, browsewrap and inquiry notice." },
    { t: "Online Contracts", k: "cl",
      q: "Priya downloaded a language-learning app. To register, she typed in her email address and pressed a button marked \"Start learning.\" Nothing on the registration screen mentioned any terms. A link labelled \"Legal\" appeared in small grey type at the very bottom of the app's home page, reachable only by scrolling past several screens of lessons. The page behind that link contained an arbitration clause. When Priya later sued the company over unauthorized charges, the company moved to compel arbitration. Which of the following statements is most accurate?",
      o: ["The clause is binding, because pressing \"Start learning\" made the terms an enforceable clickwrap.",
          "The clause is likely not binding, because it sits in a browsewrap and Priya had neither actual nor inquiry notice.",
          "The clause is binding, because the duty to read applies whether or not a user could find the terms.",
          "The clause is not binding, because the statute of frauds requires arbitration agreements to be signed by hand."],
      a: 1,
      w: "This is browsewrap, and it looks like Specht and Netscape. Under the objective theory of assent, pressing a button shows a wish to use the app, not agreement to terms that a reasonable user would never have known were there. It is not clickwrap, because nothing near the button told Priya that pressing it meant accepting any terms." },
    { t: "Battle of the Forms", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following best describes the common law's last shot rule?",
      o: ["The final form sent before performance is a counteroffer, which the other party accepts by performing.",
          "The first form sent controls, because the offeror is the master of the offer.",
          "Conflicting terms in the two forms cancel each other, and default rules fill the gap.",
          "Whichever party's lawyer speaks last at trial chooses the terms that govern."],
      a: 0,
      w: "At common law an acceptance had to be the mirror image of the offer, so a reply with different terms was a rejection and a counteroffer. When the other side went ahead and performed, that performance accepted the counteroffer, so the last form sent won. Terms cancelling each other out is the knockout rule, which belongs to the UCC, not the common law." },
    { t: "Battle of the Forms", k: "ucc",
      q: "A hotel chain sent a lamp manufacturer a purchase order for 400 desk lamps. The order said nothing about warranties. The manufacturer's acknowledgment agreed to the order and added, \"Seller disclaims all implied warranties.\" It did not make acceptance conditional on that term, and the hotel chain never responded to it. The lamps were delivered and later overheated. Both parties are merchants. Is the disclaimer part of the contract?",
      o: ["Yes, because under the last shot rule the acknowledgment was the final form sent before shipment.",
          "Yes, because between merchants every additional term becomes part of the contract unless the offeror objects.",
          "No, because the acknowledgment was a counteroffer, so no contract was ever formed.",
          "No, because a contract formed on the forms and a warranty disclaimer materially alters it."],
      a: 3,
      w: "Under section 2-207, the acknowledgment was a definite acceptance, so there is a contract on the forms. Between merchants an additional term usually slides in, but not if it materially alters the deal, and wiping out warranties is the classic material alteration. The offeror's silence is not enough to let it in, because objection is only one of three ways an additional term is kept out." },
    { t: "Battle of the Forms", k: "ucc",
      q: "Buyer, a plumbing wholesaler, sent Seller a purchase order for 2,000 brass valves at $12 each. Seller's acknowledgment listed the same goods and price but stated, \"Our acceptance is expressly conditional on Buyer's assent to the terms of this form.\" One of those terms capped Seller's liability at the purchase price. Buyer never replied. Seller shipped the valves, and Buyer accepted and paid for them. Several valves later leaked and caused water damage. Both parties are merchants. Which terms govern the contract?",
      o: ["Seller's terms, because Buyer accepted Seller's counteroffer by taking and paying for the valves.",
          "Buyer's terms only, because the offeror is the master of the offer.",
          "The terms on which both forms agree, plus UCC gap-fillers, because the contract arose from conduct.",
          "No terms at all, because forms that do not match cannot produce a contract."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Because the acknowledgment was expressly conditional on assent to its terms, it was not an acceptance, and the forms made no contract. But both sides performed, so section 2-207, subsection three, finds a contract by conduct made up of the terms the forms share plus the UCC's gap-fillers. Giving Seller its liability cap because Buyer took the goods is the common law's last shot rule, which the UCC rejects for goods." },
    { t: "Interpretation", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following best describes a course of dealing?",
      o: ["The way the parties have carried out the very contract that is now in dispute.",
          "The pattern of conduct between the same parties in their earlier transactions.",
          "The regular practice of the trade or industry in which the parties operate.",
          "The specific words the parties chose and wrote into their agreement."],
      a: 1,
      w: "A course of dealing is how these same two parties behaved in their earlier deals together. The tempting wrong answer, how they have behaved under the present contract, is course of performance. The order of priority under § 203 is express terms first, then course of performance, then course of dealing, then trade usage." },
    { t: "Interpretation", k: "cl",
      q: "A bakery signed a one-year contract with a mill for \"weekly deliveries of flour.\" The contract does not name a delivery day. For the first seven months the mill delivered every Thursday, and the bakery accepted each delivery without complaint. The bakery now insists on Monday deliveries and offers proof that mills in the region customarily deliver on Mondays. How should a court resolve the dispute?",
      o: ["For the bakery, because trade usage outranks the conduct of two individual parties.",
          "For the mill, because course of performance under this contract outranks trade usage.",
          "For the mill, because the Thursday deliveries are a course of dealing, which outranks express terms.",
          "For neither party, because the parol evidence rule bars all evidence from outside the four corners."],
      a: 1,
      w: "Seven months of Thursday deliveries accepted without complaint is a course of performance, meaning how the parties behaved under this very contract. Under § 203 that ranks above trade usage, so the regional Monday custom loses. The Thursday pattern is not a course of dealing, which means earlier contracts between the parties, and nothing ever outranks the express terms." },
    { t: "Parol Evidence Rule", k: "cl",
      q: "A homeowner hired a landscaper to rebuild her garden. They signed a two-page writing that set the price at $28,000 and the completion date at July 1. The writing has no merger clause, and the court finds it partially integrated. At trial the homeowner offers testimony that, the day before signing, the landscaper orally agreed that the price would be $25,000. How should the court rule on the testimony?",
      o: ["Admit it, because a partially integrated writing may be supplemented by any earlier term.",
          "Admit it, because an oral agreement counts as parol evidence only if it is made after signing.",
          "Exclude it, because even a partially integrated writing discharges earlier agreements that contradict it.",
          "Exclude it, because the statute of frauds requires every price term to appear in a signed writing."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Under § 213, a final writing wipes out earlier agreements that contradict it, and that is true even when the writing is only partially integrated. A price of $25,000 flatly contradicts the written $28,000, so it stays out. Partial integration only opens the door to consistent additional terms, not to a different version of a term the writing already states." },
    { t: "Parol Evidence Rule", k: "cl",
      q: "A homeowner hired a landscaper to rebuild her garden. They signed a two-page writing that set the price at $28,000 and the completion date at July 1. The writing has no merger clause, and the court finds it partially integrated. This time the homeowner offers testimony that, the day before signing, the landscaper orally promised to give her a one-hour lesson on caring for the new plants. The writing says nothing about lessons. How should the court rule on the testimony?",
      o: ["Exclude it, because nothing outside the four corners may be considered once a contract is signed.",
          "Admit it, because a partially integrated writing may be supplemented by consistent additional terms.",
          "Admit it, because contra proferentem requires every gap to be filled against the landscaper.",
          "Exclude it, because a consistent earlier term is discharged just as a contradictory one is."],
      a: 1,
      w: "Under § 210, a partially integrated writing is final on what it says, but consistent extra terms may still be added. The lesson does not contradict anything in the writing, so the testimony comes in. A consistent term within the writing's scope would be shut out only if the writing were completely integrated, and the court has found that it is not." },
    { t: "Parol Evidence Rule", k: "cl",
      q: "A homeowner hired a landscaper to rebuild her garden. They signed a two-page writing that set the price at $28,000 and the completion date at July 1. The writing has no merger clause, and the court finds it partially integrated. This time the landscaper offers testimony that, two weeks after signing, the parties orally agreed to move completion to July 15 in exchange for a $500 price reduction. The homeowner objects under the parol evidence rule. How should the court rule on the objection?",
      o: ["Sustain it, because the later agreement contradicts the completion date stated in the writing.",
          "Sustain it, because a partially integrated writing admits only consistent additional terms.",
          "Overrule it, because a completion date is never an essential term of a contract.",
          "Overrule it, because the rule reaches only agreements made before or at signing, not later modifications."],
      a: 3,
      w: "The parol evidence rule only blocks agreements made before or at the signing. A change agreed afterwards is a modification, and the rule does not touch it. It is tempting to say the new date contradicts the writing, but contradiction matters only for earlier agreements that the final writing was meant to replace." },
    { t: "Statute of Frauds", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following oral agreements is least likely to require a signed writing under the statute of frauds?",
      o: ["The sale of a small vacant lot for $900.",
          "A promise to a bank to repay a brother's loan if the brother defaults.",
          "An agreement made today to manage a shop for six months, starting ten months from now.",
          "A promise to employ a gardener for the rest of the gardener's life."],
      a: 3,
      w: "The one-year rule in § 130 asks whether the deal could possibly be fully performed within a year of its making, and a job for life can be, because a life can end within a year. The shop agreement is the trap. It lasts only six months, but the clock runs from the day the contract is made, so it cannot be finished within a year. Land sales and promises to pay another person's debt need a writing whatever the amount." },
    { t: "Statute of Frauds", k: "ucc",
      q: "A sporting goods store orally agreed to buy 200 bicycle helmets from a supplier at $30 each. The only writing is a note signed by the supplier that reads, \"Sold to the store: 120 helmets.\" The note states no price. The supplier later refused to deliver any helmets, and the store sued. Assuming the store can prove the oral agreement, what is the largest contract it can enforce against the supplier?",
      o: ["$3,600",
          "$6,000",
          "$2,400",
          "$0"],
      a: 0,
      w: "Under section 2-201, the writing must show a deal, be signed by the party being sued, and state a quantity, and the quantity in the writing is a ceiling. The supplier signed a note for 120 helmets, so the contract is enforceable up to 120 helmets at $30, which is $3,600. The missing price does not sink the note, because price can be proved by other evidence. The full 200 helmets cannot be enforced, because 80 of them are not in ink." },
    { t: "Statute of Frauds", k: "ucc",
      q: "Which of the following is NOT required for a writing to satisfy the statute of frauds for goods under UCC § 2-201(1)?",
      o: ["The signature of the party to be charged.",
          "A quantity term.",
          "The price of the goods.",
          "Some indication that a contract for sale was made."],
      a: 2,
      w: "The UCC asks for a bare writing: it must show that a deal was made, carry the signature of the party being sued, and state a quantity. Price is not required and can be supplied by other evidence. That is the contrast with the common law, where the writing must set out the essential terms." }
  ]
},
{
  id: 3,
  title: "Escape Hatches",
  blurb: "There is a deal. Can someone get out of it?",
  covers: "Unconscionability · mistake · fraud · impossibility and frustration · capacity · duress · undue influence",
  questions: [
    { t: "Unconscionability", k: "both",
      q: "Which of the following is NOT a factor that points toward a finding of unconscionability?",
      o: ["A price far above the value of what was sold",
          "A harsh term hidden deep in the fine print",
          "Terms negotiated at length by two parties who each had a lawyer",
          "A written contract in a language one party cannot read"],
      a: 2,
      w: "Unconscionability looks for an unfair process and unfair terms: an inflated price, a language barrier, unequal bargaining power, unfair surprise and hidden fine print. A deal hammered out by two advised parties has a rough balance of bargaining power, which points the other way. Remember that a hard bargain between equals still binds." },
    { t: "Unconscionability", k: "both",
      q: "A home user downloads photo-editing software and clicks \"I agree\" to a 40-page license without reading it. Page 37 states that the user owes the company $8,000 upon the tenth use of the program. After the user's tenth use, the company sues for $8,000. What is the most likely result?",
      o: ["The clause will not be enforced, because a buried term that causes unfair surprise is unconscionable.",
          "The clause will be enforced, because clicking \"I agree\" to a clickwrap makes every term binding.",
          "The whole license is void, because failing to read a contract is fraud in the inception.",
          "The clause will be enforced, because under the last shot rule the company's terms control."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Under Restatement § 208 a court may refuse to enforce a term that was unconscionable when the contract was made, and it can strike that one term and leave the rest standing. An $8,000 fee buried on page 37 is unfair surprise joined to a wildly one-sided term. Clicking \"I agree\" does show assent to the license in general, but assent does not rescue a term that shocks the conscience." },
    { t: "Mistake", k: "cl",
      q: "Which is the most accurate definition of a \"mistake\" under Restatement § 151?",
      o: ["A prediction about future events that turns out to be wrong",
          "A false statement by one party on which the other party relies",
          "A poor judgment about what the subject matter will be worth later",
          "A belief that is not in accord with the facts"],
      a: 3,
      w: "Section 151 defines a mistake as a belief that is not in accord with the facts, meaning the facts as they stand when the contract is made. A wrong prediction or a bad guess about future value is only the ordinary risk of doing business. A false statement by the other side belongs to misrepresentation, which is a different doctrine." },
    { t: "Mistake", k: "cl",
      q: "A collector sells a framed letter to a buyer for $60. Both rely on an auction house's written appraisal describing it as a printed facsimile. In fact it is a handwritten original worth $90,000. The contract says nothing about who bears the risk of authenticity. The collector learns the truth before delivery and sues to rescind. What is the most likely result?",
      o: ["The collector will lose, because a unilateral mistake requires proof that the buyer knew of the error.",
          "The collector will win, because both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption that materially affected the exchange.",
          "The collector will win, because a price that low makes the contract unconscionable.",
          "The collector will lose, because a mistake about what an item is worth is never a ground for rescission."],
      a: 1,
      w: "Under § 152, a mistake of both parties about a basic assumption that materially affects the exchange makes the contract voidable by the party who is hurt, unless that party bore the risk. Both sides believed the letter was a facsimile, and nothing put the risk on the collector. The tempting wrong answer calls this a mistake about value, but as with the cow in Sherwood and Walker, the mistake goes to the very nature of the thing sold." },
    { t: "Fraud and Misrepresentation", k: "cl",
      q: "Which is the most accurate statement of the difference between fraud in the inception and fraud in the inducement?",
      o: ["Inception makes the contract voidable at the victim's option; inducement makes the contract void from the start.",
          "Inception deceives the victim about what is being signed, so it is void; inducement persuades by lies, so it is voidable.",
          "Inception requires scienter on the part of the speaker; inducement can be committed by an honest speaker.",
          "Inception concerns lies told before the contract is signed; inducement concerns lies told after performance begins."],
      a: 1,
      w: "In fraud in the inception, the victim does not even know what she is agreeing to, so there is no real assent and the contract is void, as in Duick and Toyota. In fraud in the inducement, she knows it is a contract but was talked into it by lies, so it is voidable at her choice. The answer that swaps void and voidable is the classic trap." },
    { t: "Fraud and Misrepresentation", k: "cl",
      q: "A buyer looking at a used fishing boat asks the seller whether the hull has ever been damaged. The seller answers, \"Never.\" The seller honestly believes this, because the previous owner patched a crack below the waterline and never told him. A month after the sale the patch fails. As the best case for excuse, the buyer would assert:",
      o: ["Fraud in the inducement",
          "Fraud in the inception",
          "Unconscionability",
          "Misrepresentation"],
      a: 3,
      w: "The seller made a false statement of fact about something important, and the buyer justifiably relied on it. Under § 164 a material misrepresentation makes the contract voidable even when the speaker was honest. Fraud is the tempting answer, but fraud needs scienter, meaning the speaker knew the statement was false or did not care, and this seller believed what he said." },
    { t: "Fraud and Misrepresentation", k: "cl",
      q: "A buyer looking at a used fishing boat asks the seller whether the hull has ever been damaged. The seller answers, \"Never.\" In fact the seller cracked the hull on a rock last spring and patched it himself. A month after the sale the patch fails. The buyer's best ground for excuse is:",
      o: ["Fraud in the inducement, because the seller knowingly made a false statement of fact to bring about the sale.",
          "Fraud in the inception, because the buyer did not know the true condition of the boat.",
          "Non-disclosure, because the seller kept silent about the crack.",
          "Duress, because the buyer had no reasonable alternative to trusting the seller."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Now the seller knew the statement was false and made it to get the sale, and the buyer relied on it. That is fraud in the inducement, and the contract is voidable by the buyer. It is not non-disclosure, because the seller did not stay silent; he answered the question with a lie. It is not fraud in the inception, because the buyer knew perfectly well he was signing a contract to buy a boat." },
    { t: "Fraud and Misrepresentation", k: "cl",
      q: "A buyer looks over a used fishing boat and never asks about the hull. The seller knows he cracked the hull last spring and patched it below the waterline, where an inspection would not reveal it. The seller says nothing about it. A month after the sale the patch fails. The buyer's best argument for excuse is:",
      o: ["Misrepresentation, because the seller made a false statement of material fact.",
          "Fraud in the inception, because the buyer was deceived about the nature of the document.",
          "Non-disclosure, because the seller stayed silent about a known condition the buyer could not discover.",
          "Impracticability, because the failed patch made the boat impossible to use."],
      a: 2,
      w: "This time nobody said anything false, so the buyer cannot point to a misrepresentation. The argument has to be non-disclosure: the seller knew of a serious condition that no inspection would reveal and kept quiet. The usual rule is caveat emptor, but as in Stambovsky and Ackley, silence can be enough when the seller knows of a condition the buyer has no way to find." },
    { t: "Impossibility and Frustration", k: "cl",
      q: "A stonemason contracts to restore a particular antique fountain in a homeowner's garden for $12,000. Before work begins, lightning strikes the fountain and shatters it beyond repair. The contract has no force majeure clause. The homeowner sues the stonemason for failing to perform. What is the most likely result?",
      o: ["The stonemason will lose, because pacta sunt servanda: a promise must be kept whatever happens.",
          "The stonemason will win on mutual mistake, because neither party expected the lightning.",
          "The stonemason will win, because lightning is an Act of God and a force majeure clause is implied in every contract.",
          "The stonemason will win, because the fountain's continued existence was a basic assumption and its loss without fault made performance impracticable."],
      a: 3,
      w: "Under § 261, a duty is discharged when an event after the contract is made, through no fault of the party, makes performance impracticable, and the contract assumed that event would not happen. The fountain is like the music hall in Taylor and Caldwell: the thing essential to performance is gone. Mutual mistake does not fit, because a mistake concerns the facts at signing, and the fountain was whole on that day. A force majeure clause exists only if the parties write one." },
    { t: "Impossibility and Frustration", k: "cl",
      q: "Which statement best describes the difference between impracticability under § 261 and frustration of purpose under § 265?",
      o: ["In impracticability the performance itself is blocked; in frustration performance is still possible, but the principal purpose both parties understood has been destroyed.",
          "Impracticability excuses a party whose costs have risen; frustration excuses a party whose profits have fallen.",
          "Impracticability applies to events before the contract is signed; frustration applies to events after it is signed.",
          "Impracticability requires a force majeure clause; frustration applies only when the contract has none."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Impracticability says, I cannot do it. Frustration says, I can do it, but the whole point is gone, as when the coronation procession was cancelled in Krell and Henry. Neither one is about money: a rise in cost or a fall in profit is the ordinary risk of a fixed price deal and excuses nobody." },
    { t: "Capacity", k: "cl",
      q: "What is the least accurate statement about contracts made by minors?",
      o: ["A minor's contract is voidable, not void.",
          "A minor must pay the reasonable value of necessaries such as food and medical care.",
          "Either party may cancel the contract once the minor's age comes to light.",
          "In many states an emancipated minor who lives independently can be bound like an adult."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Under § 14 a minor's contract is voidable at the minor's option only. The adult gets no such choice, and the minor can hold the adult to the deal. The other three statements are accurate: voidable does not mean void, and necessaries and emancipation are recognised exceptions to the infancy defence." },
    { t: "Capacity", k: "cl",
      q: "A 17-year-old buys a used motorcycle from an adult dealer for $3,000. She pays $2,200 and owes the rest in installments. Three months later the engine seizes through no fault of hers, and the motorcycle is now worth $1,200. Still 17, she returns it and disaffirms. The dealer claims $1,800 for depreciation. Under the general rule, how much must the dealer refund?",
      o: ["$3,000",
          "$2,200",
          "$1,200",
          "$400"],
      a: 1,
      w: "A minor who disaffirms hands back whatever is left and recovers what she paid, which is $2,200. As in Halbman and Lemke, she owes nothing for use or depreciation, because charging her would enforce the contract by the back door. The $400 figure comes from subtracting the $1,800 of depreciation, which is exactly the deduction the rule forbids. A motorcycle, like a car, is not a necessary." },
    { t: "Duress", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following is NOT an improper threat under Restatement § 176?",
      o: ["A threat to file a lawsuit, made in good faith, to collect a debt the creditor honestly believes is owed",
          "A threat to commit a tort against the other party",
          "A threat to have the other party criminally prosecuted unless he signs",
          "A bad faith threat to break an existing contract with the other party unless she agrees to pay more"],
      a: 0,
      w: "Section 176 treats a threat as improper when it threatens a crime or a tort, criminal prosecution, a lawsuit brought in bad faith, or a breach of the duty of good faith under an existing contract. Telling someone to pay what you honestly believe is owed or you will sue is a lawful use of the courts. The word that matters is bad faith; without it, a threat to sue is just hard bargaining." },
    { t: "Duress", k: "cl",
      q: "A printer agrees to print 10,000 festival programs for an organizer for $6,000. A month before the festival, the printer announces that it will stop work unless the organizer agrees to pay $9,000. The organizer knows that three other printers in town could finish the job on time for about $6,500, but she agrees in order to avoid the hassle and pays the $9,000. After the festival she sues to recover the extra $3,000, claiming economic duress. On the duress claim, what is the most likely result?",
      o: ["The organizer will win, because the threat to breach was improper, and an improper threat is all that duress requires.",
          "The organizer will win, because the printer's demand was unfair persuasion amounting to undue influence.",
          "The printer will win, because duress is limited to threats of physical harm and cannot be claimed by a business.",
          "The printer will win, because the organizer had a reasonable alternative: hire another printer and sue for damages."],
      a: 3,
      w: "Under § 175, duress needs an improper threat and no reasonable alternative. The printer's threat may well have been improper, but the organizer could have hired one of three other printers and sued for the difference, so she had a way out. Compare Austin Instrument and Loral, where the buyer called ten suppliers and none could deliver in time. An improper threat alone is not enough." },
    { t: "Undue Influence", k: "cl",
      q: "Walter, an 82-year-old widower, is mentally sharp. Two days after he comes home from the hospital, his long-time financial adviser and the adviser's business partner arrive at his house at 10 p.m. They urge him to sign, that night, a contract selling his lake cabin to the adviser for half its value. They say the offer ends at midnight and that there is no need to bother his daughter or his lawyer. Nobody threatens him. Walter signs. In court, Walter's best defense is:",
      o: ["Duress, because an improper threat left him no reasonable alternative.",
          "Lack of capacity, because his age and recent illness make the contract voidable.",
          "Undue influence, because a trusted adviser used unfair persuasion: multiple persuaders, an unusual time and place, and no chance to get advice.",
          "Fraud in the inception, because he signed without reading the contract closely."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Under § 177, undue influence is unfair persuasion of a person who is justified in trusting the persuader. Walter trusted his adviser, and the warning signs from Odorizzi are all here: two persuaders, late at night in his home, insistence on signing at once, and no time for a lawyer. Duress fails because nobody made a threat. Capacity fails because Walter was mentally sharp, and as the Burren case shows, competence does not defeat undue influence." }
  ]
},
{
  id: 4,
  title: "The Whole Board",
  blurb: "Performance, remedies and third parties: what happens when the deal goes wrong.",
  covers: "Damages · limits on damages · specific performance · performance standards · UCC remedies · third parties",
  questions: [
    { t: "Damages", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the three interests that contract remedies protect under Restatement §344?",
      o: ["The expectation interest, meaning the benefit of the bargain.",
          "The reliance interest, meaning the out-of-pocket loss caused by trusting the promise.",
          "The punitive interest, meaning a sum set to deter future breaches.",
          "The restitution interest, meaning a benefit conferred on the other party."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Section 344 protects expectation, reliance and restitution. Contract damages compensate the injured party, and section 355 rules out punitive damages unless the breach is also a tort. Reliance may sound like the odd one out because it is the fallback measure, but it is one of the three." },
    { t: "Damages", k: "cl",
      q: "A landscaper agreed to build a stone patio for a homeowner for $30,000. The landscaper expected the whole job to cost $22,000 in labour and materials. After the landscaper had spent $9,000 on the job, and before the homeowner had paid anything, the homeowner repudiated the contract. The landscaper stopped work at once and could not reuse any of the materials. How much would the landscaper collect under the expectation (benefit of the bargain) measure?",
      o: ["$30,000",
          "$8,000",
          "$9,000",
          "$17,000"],
      a: 3,
      w: "Expectation puts the landscaper where full performance would have. That is the $8,000 profit plus the $9,000 already spent, which is $17,000. You reach the same figure by taking the $30,000 price and subtracting the $13,000 of cost the landscaper avoided by stopping. The full $30,000 ignores the costs saved, and $9,000 alone is only the reliance measure." },
    { t: "Limits on Damages", k: "cl",
      q: "A caterer left a portable generator with a repair shop, which promised to return it by Friday. The caterer did not say what the generator was used for. The shop returned it the following Wednesday. On Saturday, when the caterer called to complain, she told the shop that she was missing a weekend festival at which she would have cleared $6,000. She sues the shop for the $6,000. What is the most likely outcome?",
      o: ["The caterer will recover, because expectation damages cover every loss that the breach in fact caused.",
          "The caterer will not recover, because the shop had no reason to foresee the festival loss when the contract was made.",
          "The caterer will not recover, because lost profits can never be proved with enough certainty.",
          "The caterer will recover, because the shop learned of the festival before it returned the generator."],
      a: 1,
      w: "Under section 351 and Hadley and Baxendale, the breaching party pays only for loss it had reason to foresee when the contract was made. The shop was told nothing about the festival at that time. Telling the shop on Saturday, after the deal was done, comes too late, because foreseeability is judged at the moment of contracting and not at the moment of breach." },
    { t: "Limits on Damages", k: "cl",
      q: "Why will a court refuse to enforce a stipulated damages clause that it classifies as a penalty?",
      o: ["Because contract remedies exist to compensate the injured party, not to punish the party in breach.",
          "Because parties have no freedom to agree on their remedies in advance.",
          "Because the doctrine of avoidable consequences forbids fixing damages before a breach occurs.",
          "Because stipulated damages are a form of caveat emptor that binds only merchants."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Parties may fix damages in advance, but under section 356 only at a reasonable forecast of the loss. An unreasonably large sum is a penalty, and contract law compensates. It does not punish. It is wrong to say parties cannot agree on remedies at all, because a fair forecast is enforced." },
    { t: "Specific Performance", k: "cl",
      q: "Which of the following is NOT a factor a court weighs under Restatement §360 in deciding whether money damages are an adequate remedy?",
      o: ["How hard it is to prove the amount of the loss with reasonable certainty.",
          "How hard it is to buy a suitable substitute with the money awarded.",
          "How likely it is that an award of damages could actually be collected.",
          "How deliberate the breach was."],
      a: 3,
      w: "Section 360 asks three things about money: is the loss hard to value, is a substitute hard to purchase, and are damages unlikely to be collected. The breaching party's state of mind is not on that list. Specific performance turns on whether money is inadequate, not on how badly the other side behaved." },
    { t: "Specific Performance", k: "cl",
      q: "A regional theatre company signed a well-known stage director to direct its three autumn productions for a fee of $90,000. The contract contained no promise by the director not to work for anyone else. Two weeks before rehearsals were to begin, the director told the company she was leaving to direct a film instead. The company, which had already sold season tickets on the strength of her name, asks a court to order her to direct the productions as promised. What is the most likely outcome?",
      o: ["The court will grant specific performance, because the director's talent is unique and no substitute exists.",
          "The court will grant specific performance, because the company relied on her promise when it sold tickets.",
          "The court will deny specific performance, because a court will not compel and supervise personal services.",
          "The court will deny specific performance, because the contract was to last less than one year."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Personal service is the classic case where specific performance is refused. A court cannot supervise the quality of the work, and forcing a person to labour looks like involuntary servitude. Her talent may well be unique, but uniqueness alone does not get an order to perform, so the company is left to claim money." },
    { t: "Substantial Performance", k: "cl",
      q: "Which is the most accurate statement of the common law doctrine of substantial performance?",
      o: ["A party whose performance departs from the contract in any respect may recover nothing.",
          "A party who substantially performs may recover the contract price, less damages for the defect.",
          "A party who substantially performs has not breached, so nothing is deducted from the price.",
          "A buyer of goods must accept any tender that substantially conforms to the contract."],
      a: 1,
      w: "Under Jacob and Youngs and Kent, a party who substantially performs gets paid, minus damages for the defect. The tempting mistake is to think substantial performance excuses the breach. It does not: the shortfall is still a breach and the builder still pays for it. And for goods the rule is perfect tender, not substantial performance." },
    { t: "Substantial Performance", k: "cl",
      q: "A contractor built a swimming pool for a homeowner for $80,000. The contract called for a depth of eight feet at the deep end. Through an innocent measuring error the pool is three inches shallower. It is fully usable for everything the homeowner wanted, and its market value is $500 lower than promised. Rebuilding to the specified depth would cost $45,000. The homeowner refuses to pay the final $20,000 instalment. What is the most likely outcome?",
      o: ["The contractor recovers nothing, because under the perfect tender rule any nonconformity justifies refusing payment.",
          "The contractor recovers the full $20,000, because an innocent mistake is not a breach.",
          "The contractor recovers nothing, because the $45,000 cost of completion exceeds the unpaid balance.",
          "The contractor recovers $19,500, because the cost to rebuild is clearly out of proportion to the loss in value."],
      a: 3,
      w: "The contractor substantially performed: the slip was innocent, the defect is petty and the pool serves its purpose. Section 348 normally awards the cost to fix, but when that cost is clearly out of proportion to the loss in value, the owner gets only the loss in value, here $500. The perfect tender rule is a UCC rule for goods and has no place in a construction contract." },
    { t: "Perfect Tender", k: "ucc",
      q: "A café owner ordered 60 identical oak chairs from a furniture maker, to arrive in a single shipment on May 1. The shipment arrived on May 1. Inspecting it that day, the owner found that four chairs were stained a noticeably darker shade than the contract specified. She immediately notified the furniture maker that she was rejecting the entire shipment. Was the rejection rightful?",
      o: ["Yes, because goods that fail to conform to the contract in any respect may be rejected in whole.",
          "No, because the furniture maker substantially performed and the owner's remedy is a price deduction.",
          "No, because a buyer may reject only the nonconforming units and must keep the rest.",
          "Yes, but only because the darker stain substantially impaired the value of the whole shipment."],
      a: 0,
      w: "This is a single delivery of goods, so section 2-601 applies. If the goods fail to conform in any respect, the buyer may reject all, accept all, or accept some commercial units and reject the rest. Substantial performance is the common law rule for services and construction, and substantial impairment is not required before acceptance." },
    { t: "Perfect Tender", k: "ucc",
      q: "Same facts as the previous question, except that the contract called for the 60 chairs to arrive in three separate lots of 20, on May 1, June 1 and July 1. The four darker chairs arrived in the first lot, and the furniture maker promptly offered to swap them for correctly stained chairs within the week. May the café owner reject the first lot?",
      o: ["Yes, because the perfect tender rule applies to every delivery of goods.",
          "No, because under an instalment contract a lot may be rejected only if the defect substantially impairs its value and cannot be cured.",
          "No, because the owner accepted the chairs by inspecting them.",
          "Yes, because the furniture maker is a merchant and is held to a stricter standard."],
      a: 1,
      w: "Separate deliveries make this an instalment contract, and section 2-612 switches perfect tender off. The buyer may reject an instalment only if the defect substantially impairs its value and cannot be cured. Four chairs that the seller offers to swap within the week do not meet that test. Inspecting goods is not accepting them; a buyer accepts by failing to reject after a fair chance to inspect." },
    { t: "UCC Remedies", k: "ucc",
      q: "A brewery contracted to buy 500 pounds of hops from a grower at $8 per pound. The grower failed to deliver. On the day the brewery learned of the breach, the market price of hops was $10 per pound. Acting promptly and in good faith, the brewery bought 500 pounds from another grower at $11 per pound. Ignoring incidental and consequential damages, how much may the brewery recover under UCC §2-712?",
      o: ["$5,500",
          "$1,000",
          "$1,500",
          "$4,000"],
      a: 2,
      w: "A buyer who covers in good faith and without unreasonable delay recovers the cover price minus the contract price. That is $5,500 minus $4,000, which is $1,500. The $1,000 figure is the market price formula in section 2-713, and that is for a buyer who does not cover. Cover has to be reasonable, not the cheapest deal available." },
    { t: "UCC Remedies", k: "ucc",
      q: "Under the UCC, which of the following best describes a lost volume seller?",
      o: ["A seller with enough supply to have made both the original sale and the later resale, so that the breach cost it one sale's profit.",
          "A seller that resold the goods for less than the contract price and claims the difference.",
          "A seller that seeks cover by buying substitute goods from another supplier.",
          "A seller whose custom goods cannot be resold to anyone and that sues for the full price."],
      a: 0,
      w: "A lost volume seller could have supplied both buyers, so reselling the goods at the same price does not make it whole. Under section 2-708, subsection two, and Neri and Retail Marine, it recovers its lost profit. The contract price minus resale price formula is the ordinary resale remedy in section 2-706, and cover is a buyer's remedy, not a seller's." },
    { t: "Third-Party Beneficiaries", k: "cl",
      q: "Which is the least accurate statement about third-party beneficiaries?",
      o: ["An intended beneficiary may enforce the promise even though it signed nothing and gave no consideration.",
          "The promisor may raise against the beneficiary the defences it would have had against the promisee.",
          "Anyone who would have been better off had the contract been performed may sue to enforce it.",
          "A creditor of the promisee, whom the promisor has agreed to pay, is a classic intended beneficiary."],
      a: 2,
      w: "Benefit is not the test. Intent is. Under section 302 only an intended beneficiary can sue, and someone who merely happens to gain is incidental and gets nothing. The other statements are accurate: Lawrence and Fox let a creditor sue without privity or consideration, and under section 309 the promisor keeps its defences." },
    { t: "Third-Party Beneficiaries", k: "cl",
      q: "A developer hired a builder to construct a hotel on the developer's land by June 1. The owner of a restaurant next door, expecting hotel guests, hired extra staff for the summer. The builder abandoned the job and the hotel never opened. The restaurant owner sues the builder for breach of the construction contract. What is the most likely outcome?",
      o: ["The restaurant owner will win, because performance of the contract would have benefited her.",
          "The restaurant owner will win, because she relied on the contract when she hired extra staff.",
          "The restaurant owner will lose, because she is an incidental beneficiary whom the parties did not intend to benefit.",
          "The restaurant owner will lose, because a person who is not in privity can never enforce a contract."],
      a: 2,
      w: "The developer and the builder made their deal for themselves, not for the neighbour. She would have gained from it, but a happy side effect makes her only an incidental beneficiary, and incidental beneficiaries cannot sue. The privity answer reaches the right result for the wrong reason, because intended beneficiaries can enforce contracts they never signed." },
    { t: "Assignment and Delegation", k: "cl",
      q: "A homeowner hired a roofing company to replace her roof with standard asphalt shingles for $18,000. The roofing company, which was overbooked, handed the job to a second roofer without asking the homeowner. The second roofer did the work badly, and the roof leaks. The homeowner sues the original roofing company. What is the most likely outcome?",
      o: ["The company is liable, because a party that delegates a duty stays responsible unless the other side agrees to a novation.",
          "The company is not liable, because it validly delegated the duty and the second roofer took its place.",
          "The company is liable, because duties under a service contract can never be delegated.",
          "The company is not liable, because the homeowner's only claim is as a beneficiary against the second roofer."],
      a: 0,
      w: "Under section 318 a duty like ordinary roofing can be delegated, but delegating does not get you off the hook. The delegator stays liable if the delegate performs badly. Only a novation, a new agreement in which the homeowner consents to the swap, would release the original company. It is wrong to say service duties can never be delegated; the bar is for duties that depend on personal skill or trust." }
  ]
}
];
